📘 Chapter 1: Introduction & Project Synopsis

• 1.1 Background: Visually impaired individuals face daily challenges navigating unfamiliar environments. Traditional aids like white canes cannot identify what an obstacle is or where it is shifting in real time.
• 1.2 Problem Statement: Developing a system that processes live video feeds, extracts meaningful details about objects, calculates where they are relative to the user, and announces them without lag on ordinary computers.
• 1.3 Project Objective:
	• To implement a low-latency real-time tracking pipeline using the YOLOv4-Tiny network model.

• 
	• To partition visual frames horizontally into Left, Center, and Right zones for clear directional cues.
	• To use asynchronous audio threads to prevent voice queues from freezing the camera feed.

📚 Chapter 2: Literature Review & Background Study

• 2.1 Conventional Systems: Traditional object recognition models like standard R-CNN or full YOLOv4 provide high accuracy but demand heavy graphical processing units (GPUs), making them impractical for affordable or portable devices.
• 2.2 Selected Approach: YOLOv4-Tiny compresses neural network layers from over 100 down to just 29 layers. This modification drastically lowers the mathematical operations required, allowing the software to achieve true 30+ Frames Per Second (FPS) directly on standard laptop CPUs.

